   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Posted accidentally before I had finished writing. Sorry about that! <div><br></div><div>(continuing...)</div><div>I&#39;ve been using this approach successfully thus far, however this error occurred randomly from time to time. Now I just can&#39;t get rid of the error. I&#39;ve tried changing the names of the factors, but it still didn&#39;t work.</div><div><br></div><div>What could be causing it?</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br><br>Em quarta-feira, 18 de outubro de 2017 10:20:04 UTC-2, Pedro Rocha  escreveu:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hello everyone,<div><br></div><div>I&#39;m having a strange error trying to subset my landmark coordinates for a set of specimens. I attached a data frame with the factors I&#39;d like to use and also the tps file with the coordinates.</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><font color=\"#660066\"><span style=\"color:#000\">land </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> readland</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">tps</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;generic coordinates.tps&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> specID </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;ID&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></font><span style=\"color:#606\">Groups</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">read</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">table</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;Groups.txt&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"color:#000\"> h</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sep</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;\\t&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></font></div></code></div><div><br></div>When I subset using the columns Groups$Sp and Groups$Loc, it works perfectly. However, when I try subset using Groups$Ind, it returns an error:</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><div>&gt; land.sp &lt;- coords.subset(A=land, group=Groups$Sp) # By species</div><div>&gt; land.loc &lt;- coords.subset(A=land, group=Groups$Loc) # By locality</div><div>&gt; land.ind &lt;- coords.subset(A=land, group=Groups$Ind) # By individual</div><div>Error in dimnames(specimens) &lt;- `*vtmp*` : &#39;dimnames&#39; must be a list</div></div></code></div><br>I&#39;ve been using this approach successfully thus far, however this error occurred randomly from time to time. Now I just can&#39;ve tried changing the names of the factors, but it still didn&#39;t work. <br><br></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
